<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/expert/reliability-and-safety-engineering">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the capstone of the Engineering track's safety thread. You have already met the
    physics of loading in Statics and structures and Forces, beams and bridges, the way
    materials give way in Materials selection, and the feedback loops that hold systems on
    target in Control systems. Here those threads close into the discipline that decides
    whether engineered systems fail safely or fail catastrophically.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>apply the hierarchy of controls in its correct priority order and justify why PPE ranks last;</li>
    <li>compute a time-weighted average exposure and explain why monitoring integrates over the whole shift;</li>
    <li>read gas-monitor and electrical-safety numbers — LEL alarm thresholds, RCD trip currents — as engineering requirements rather than trivia;</li>
    <li>calculate series and parallel reliability, and explain why independence, not duplication, is what redundancy actually buys;</li>
    <li>name the analysis toolkit — FMEA, fault trees, probabilistic risk assessment — and the assumptions each method quietly depends on.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Safety engineering is <strong>designing for the certainty that things fail</strong> — and for
    the certainty that people err. It is engineering's moral core: the physics lesson's safety
    factors, grown into a discipline of redundancy, analysis and culture. The FE syllabus this
    course follows names six knowledge areas — industrial hygiene, safety equipment, gas
    monitoring, electrical safety, confined spaces, hazard communication — and they share one
    architecture: recognise the hazard, rank it, control it by the hierarchy (eliminate,
    substitute, engineer, administrate, protect), communicate the residue, and verify with
    numbers. Reliability engineering is the quantitative half of the same discipline: the
    mathematics that tells you how often things fail and what redundancy buys. Together they
    turn "be careful" into an auditable engineering method — which is the difference between
    industries that kill people occasionally and industries that almost never do.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="fail-safe thinking, redundancy, the toolkit, human factors" widget="equationStep" config={safetyCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the panel walks the discipline's spine in four steps —
    the fail-safe design question ("what happens when it fails?"), redundancy and its
    common-cause trap, the analysis toolkit (FMEA, fault trees, probabilistic risk
    assessment), and human factors as system design rather than operator blame. Step through
    them in order and ask of each: what does this buy, and what assumption does it quietly
    depend on? The second answer — independence for redundancy, audited assumptions for
    probabilistic risk assessment — is where the discipline earns every number it publishes.
  </p>

  <h2>Industrial hygiene: the dose makes the poison</h2>
  <p>
    Industrial hygiene is anticipatory toxicology: identify agents (chemical, physical,
    biological), measure exposure, compare against limits, control before harm. The
    mechanism is the <strong>dose–response relationship</strong> — toxicity depends on dose,
    route (inhalation, skin absorption, ingestion) and duration — operationalised through
    exposure limits: the TLV-TWA (threshold limit value, time-weighted average over an
    8-hour shift) and the ceiling limit (never exceed, for fast-acting agents). A worked
    calculation: a worker exposed to carbon monoxide at 40 ppm for 4 hours and 10 ppm for 4
    hours has a TWA of (40 × 4 + 10 × 4)/8 = 25 ppm — exactly the ACGIH TLV for CO, and a
    different conclusion than either reading alone suggests, which is why monitoring
    integrates over time. The specialty agents each carry their own machinery: <strong>carcinogens</strong>
    are classified by evidence strength (IARC Group 1 = established human carcinogen —
    asbestos, benzene, formaldehyde) and controlled to the lowest achievable, because no
    threshold is assumed; <strong>radiation</strong> exposure is managed under ALARA (as low
    as reasonably achievable) with dose measured in sieverts and the distinction that alpha
    particles stop on skin but are lethal inhaled; <strong>biohazards</strong> are managed by
    biosafety level (BSL-1 routine microbes to BSL-4 Ebola), with containment matching
    infectivity; and <strong>half-life</strong> decides both decay strategy (store
    radioactive waste for ~10 half-lives to a 0.1% residue) and biological clearance (lead's
    decades in bone versus alcohol's hours).
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> industrial hygiene measures the hazard — dose, route, duration, and the exposure limits that turn measurement into a decision. Next comes the ranking machinery that decides which control to buy first.</p>

  <h2>Basic safety equipment: the hierarchy of controls</h2>
  <p>
    Safety equipment is designed inside a strict priority order — the <strong>hierarchy of
    controls</strong>: eliminate the hazard, substitute it, engineer it away, administrate,
    and only then <em>personal protective equipment</em>. PPE is last because it protects one
    person, only when worn correctly, against a hazard everyone else still faces; engineering
    controls protect everyone, always. The canonical engineering devices each encode a
    fail-safe philosophy. A <strong>pressure-relief valve</strong> opens automatically at set
    pressure and is sized for the worst credible overpressure scenario (fire engulfment sets
    the sizing case per API standards) — it must never be the design's only defence, but it
    must always work. <strong>Emergency shutoffs</strong> isolate energy at the source,
    reachable without approaching the hazard. <strong>Fire prevention and control</strong>
    starts with the fire triangle (fuel, oxidiser, ignition — remove any leg) and continues
    through suppression matched to the fire class (water on an electrical fire kills the
    firefighter; CO₂ or dry powder for live equipment; Class D agents for metal fires —
    water makes burning magnesium worse). The case that grades the whole topic is Bhopal
    (1984): the methyl-isocyanate plant's relief system and flare tower existed but its
    refrigeration (which would have limited the leak) and scrubber (which would have
    neutralised it) were offline — thousands died because the engineered layers had been
    economised away. Hardware is the cheap part; keeping it live is the discipline.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "PPE is the first thing you reach for, so it's the first
    line of defence." It is the last. A respirator that protects one worker is ranked below
    a ventilation hood that protects the room, which ranks below substituting the solvent
    entirely. Safety audits that begin with "are the gloves available?" have already skipped
    four stronger controls — and regulators cite the hierarchy precisely because teams
    instinctively start at the bottom of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Gas detection and monitoring: what you cannot smell can kill you</h2>
  <p>
    Atmospheric monitoring exists because human senses are calibrated for the Pleistocene,
    not the plant. The monitored gases and their logic: <strong>oxygen</strong> (normal
    20.9%; below 19.5% is "oxygen-deficient" — impairment arrives near 12–16%, unconsciousness
    near 10%); <strong>carbon monoxide</strong> — odourless, binds haemoglobin ~200× more
    strongly than oxygen, and its symptoms (headache, confusion) arrive before the victim
    knows; <strong>hydrogen sulphide</strong> — the olfactory trap, noticeable at well under
    10 ppm but <em>paralysing the sense of smell</em> at higher concentrations, so "it stopped
    smelling" means it got worse; <strong>methane and combustibles</strong> — managed against
    the explosive range (methane: 5–15% in air), which is why monitors report percent of the
    lower explosive limit (LEL) with alarms at 10–20%; <strong>carbon dioxide</strong> (a
    simple asphyxiant that also displaces air in fermentation cellars and brew pits);
    <strong>radon</strong> — the radioactive soil gas and the second-leading cause of lung
    cancer after smoking, managed in buildings with an action level (US EPA: 4 pCi/L, about
    150 Bq/m³). The instruments are electrochemical cells, catalytic pellistors and infrared
    sensors, packaged as fixed plant monitors with ventilation interlocks and as personal
    four-gas monitors — because the atmosphere that kills is usually the one that changed
    since yesterday.
  </p>

  <h2>Electrical safety: current, path and time</h2>
  <p>
    The first principle is that <strong>current kills, not voltage</strong>: the fibrillation
    threshold through the chest is on the order of tens of milliamps — far below the amps a
    household circuit supplies — so severity is set by body resistance (dry skin: ~100,000
    ohms; wet: ~1,000, a hundredfold increase in current for the same touch voltage), the
    current path (hand-to-hand crosses the heart), and duration. The defences are layered
    accordingly: <strong>isolation and insulation</strong> (the first line),
    <strong>earthing/grounding and protective devices</strong> (RCDs/GFCIs trip at leakage
    currents of roughly 5–30 mA depending on standard — faster and at lower current than
    fibrillation requires), <strong>lockout–tagout</strong> (the administrative control that
    makes de-energised mean de-energised: the person working applies their own lock, so
    re-energisation requires their key), and <strong>arc-flash management</strong> — the
    specialist hazard of high-energy switchgear, where an arc reaches ~20,000 K (hotter than
    the sun's surface) and incident energy in cal/cm², calculated per IEEE 1584, sets PPE
    categories. The worked case that motivates all of it: a 240-volt hand-to-hand contact
    through wet skin delivers roughly 240 mA — nearly ten times the fibrillation threshold —
    which is why "only 240 volts" is a sentence that appears in accident reports.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> whether the threat is chemical or electrical, the logic is identical — quantify the hazard, then layer defences so that no single failure, sense or device stands between a worker and harm. The next section applies that logic to the spaces where keeping those defences honest is hardest.</p>

  <h2>Confined spaces and ventilation rates</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>permit-required confined space</strong> (tank, silo, pit, sewer, ship
    compartment) is defined by hazard, not size: limited entry/exit, not designed for
    continuous occupancy, and capable of hazardous atmosphere, engulfment or entrapment. The
    protocol is strict because the failure mode is a cascade: test the atmosphere
    <em>before entry and in the right order</em> — oxygen first (combustible-gas meters need
    oxygen to read correctly), then flammables, then toxics — ventilate mechanically, never
    trust the senses, and station an attendant outside who <em>does not enter</em> to rescue
    (roughly 60% of confined-space fatalities are would-be rescuers, multi-fatality by
    instinct). The atmosphere traps deserve their own respect: <strong>nitrogen and CO₂ are
    not "safe" gases — they are asphyxiants</strong> (a nitrogen-purged vessel has no smell
    and no warning; unconsciousness arrives in a few breaths below ~10% oxygen — the reason
    confined-space entry uses supplied air, not "common sense"); and <strong>engulfment</strong>
    (grain, sand, sludge) kills by obstruction and crush in seconds. Ventilation is the
    standing engineering control — dilution and displacement of contaminants, with rates set
    by the hazard calculation (for example, ensuring weld fume concentrations stay under
    exposure limits) — and the permit is the administrative control that makes the checks
    non-optional: signature, time, gas readings recorded, rescue plan staged.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "if the space smelled dangerous, you'd know." The three
    deadliest confined-space atmospheres are precisely the ones smell cannot referee:
    nitrogen and CO₂ have no odour at all, CO is odourless, and H₂S anaesthetises the nose
    exactly when it becomes lethal. Instrumentation is the only functional nose in
    industrial hygiene — which is why "test before entry" is written in blood, not in
    regulation style.
  </p>

  <h2>Hazard communication: the paperwork that works</h2>
  <p>
    Hazard communication (HazCom) is the system that carries chemical safety information
    from manufacturer to user, and it is built on the UN's <strong>Globally Harmonised
    System</strong>: standardised label elements (pictograms — flame, skull-and-crossbones,
    corrosion — plus signal words "Danger"/"Warning" and hazard statements) and the
    <strong>Safety Data Sheet</strong> in a fixed 16-section format (identification, hazards,
    composition, first aid, firefighting, accidental release, handling/storage, exposure
    controls, physical properties, stability, toxicology, ecology, disposal, transport,
    regulation, other). Concentration information on labels and SDSs is how a worker knows
    the difference between a 3% hydrogen-peroxide antiseptic and the 30% laboratory
    oxidiser with the same name; fire ratings (the NFPA 704 diamond's 0–4 blue/red/yellow/
    white quadrants — health, flammability, instability, special) compress a hazard
    assessment to a glance readable in an emergency. The system's deeper function is
    institutional: the label is the contract that makes the exposure limits of the hygiene
    section, the PPE of the equipment section, and the storage segregation of the fire
    section all legible to the person holding the drum — "right to know" as an engineering
    control, not a poster.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the six hazard areas share one architecture — recognise, rank, control, communicate, verify. Now the discipline acquires numbers: reliability is the mathematics of how often the controls themselves fail.</p>

  <h2>Reliability engineering: putting numbers on failure</h2>
  <p>
    Reliability is safety's quantitative twin: the probability a component performs its
    function for a stated time. Failure rates trace the <strong>bath-tub curve</strong>
    (early-life failures, a long random-failure plateau, wear-out at the end), and for the
    plateau the exponential model gives <strong>MTBF</strong> (mean time between failures)
    and availability A = MTBF/(MTBF + MTTR). System structure does the arithmetic: components
    in <em>series</em> multiply unreliability (ten parts at 99.9% reliability each yield about
    99.0% for the whole — 1% of a million operations is 10,000 failures), components in
    <em>parallel</em> multiply reliability (two independent 90%-reliable units: 99%), and
    the design question is always whether the redundancy is <em>independent</em> — common-cause
    failure (one maintenance error, one shared software bug, one flooded bay) defeats
    duplication, which is why the 737 MAX's single-sensor dependence on MCAS and Boeing's
    assumption that pilots would respond in seconds is the modern case study in hidden
    common mode. The analysis toolkit — FMEA (list failure modes, score severity ×
    likelihood × detectability), fault-tree analysis (top event down to basic events, AND/
    OR gates), and probabilistic risk assessment — turns intuitions into ranked, budgetable
    numbers. The space shuttle is PRA's moral: pre-Challenger, management cited roughly 1
    failure in 100,000 flights; Feynman argued about 1 in 100; the operational record came
    out at 2 in 135 — a factor of a thousand between the spreadsheet and the physics, which
    is exactly why PRA results must be audited against the assumptions feeding them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "adding a backup doubles the safety." Only if the backup
    fails independently: two parallel 90%-reliable units reach 99% — a tenfold improvement,
    not a doubling — but a shared power feed, shared firmware or shared maintenance
    procedure can collapse the whole stack to single-digit gains. Redundancy engineering is
    10% duplication and 90% independence analysis, which is why diversity (different
    suppliers, different physical principles, different failure modes) is priced above
    copies.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A worker is exposed to 60 ppm of a toxic gas for 2 hours and 20 ppm for 6 hours of an 8-hour shift. What is the time-weighted average exposure, and why does the TWA rather than either reading alone decide compliance?</li>
    <li>Two independent units, each 90% reliable over a mission, are placed in parallel. What reliability does the redundant pair achieve — and what single change would collapse most of that gain?</li>
    <li>Why is personal protective equipment ranked last in the hierarchy of controls, even though it is the control workers interact with most directly?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) TWA = (60 × 2 + 20 × 6)/8 = 30 ppm. Compliance is set by
    the integrated dose because toxicity depends on dose and duration — a dangerous peak and
    a quiet morning can average to the same exposure, which is why monitors integrate over
    the shift instead of sampling once. (2) The pair reaches 1 − 0.1 × 0.1 = 99% — a tenfold
    cut in failure probability, not a doubling. The gain collapses if the redundancy shares a
    failure mode: one common power feed, one shared firmware bug or one maintenance error can
    defeat both units at once, which is why diversity is priced above copies. (3) Because PPE
    protects one person, only when worn correctly, against a hazard that still exists for
    everyone else; engineering controls protect everyone, always. The hierarchy ranks controls
    by how much of the hazard they remove, not by how close they sit to the worker.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Safety engineering designs for the certainty of failure: the question is never "can it fail?" but "what happens when it does?"</li>
    <li>The hierarchy of controls ranks eliminate, substitute, engineer, administrate, protect — PPE is last because it protects one person, one error at a time.</li>
    <li>Exposure limits (TLV-TWA, ceiling values), ALARA and biosafety levels turn measurement into defensible decisions.</li>
    <li>Smell is not an instrument: the deadliest atmospheres (CO, nitrogen, H₂S) are exactly the ones human senses cannot referee — test before entry.</li>
    <li>Current, path and time set electrical severity; RCDs and lockout–tagout defend the margins.</li>
    <li>Series multiplies unreliability, parallel multiplies reliability — but only if the redundancy is genuinely independent.</li>
    <li>FMEA, fault trees and probabilistic risk assessment convert intuition into ranked, budgetable numbers — which must be audited against their own assumptions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The natural continuation within Engineering is Systems engineering, which zooms out from
    component failure to the design of whole systems and their life cycles — the frame in
    which safety cases are written and reviewed. For the "keep it within safe limits" half of
    the discipline engineered in practice, continue with the advanced lesson Control systems.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Human factors and the systems view.</strong> The field's decisive shift was from
    blaming operators to designing around them: Reason's Swiss-cheese model (accidents need
    holes in several defences to align), the no-blame reporting systems that made aviation
    the safest transport mode (ASRS and its imitators collect hundreds of thousands of
    confidential near-miss reports), checklists as cognitive prosthetics (the aviation
    pre-flight, imported into surgery by Gawande with measured mortality reductions), and
    Leveson's STAMP, which recasts safety as a <em>control problem</em> — accidents as
    inadequate control of the system's behaviour, including its organisational behaviour.
    The common thread: "human error" is a symptom, and the engineering product is the design
    that makes the error impossible, detectable or recoverable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Quantitative frontiers.</strong> Modern PRA layers event trees and fault trees
    with uncertainty propagation (Monte Carlo over failure distributions), human-reliability
    analysis (technique for human error rate prediction, with error probabilities spanning
    orders of magnitude by context), and common-cause beta factors. The live debates:
    digital systems break the classical independence assumptions (one line of software runs
    everywhere); climate change moves the environmental boundary conditions PRA assumed
    static (Fukushima's tsunami heights were probabilities from a stationary world); and
    autonomous systems force reliability analysis onto learning components whose failure
    rates are distributions over <em>inputs</em> rather than time — the safety case of the
    next decades is being drafted now, and it looks more like the AI evaluation chapter
    than the bathtub curve.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Safety-II and resilience engineering.</strong> The newest school inverts the
    question: instead of studying why things fail, study why things usually succeed — safety
    as the presence of adaptive capacity, not the absence of accidents. Its practical
    instruments are leading indicators (near-miss reports, defences-in-depth audits,
    workload and drift measurements) rather than the lagging injury counts that
    statistically stop arriving in mature systems precisely when complacency grows
    (Vaughan's "normalisation of deviance" at Challenger is the canonical mechanism). The
    mature organisation, like the mature engineer, treats the absence of accidents as
    incomplete information about risk — never as evidence of safety.
  </p>

